## Examples

### Plain text email

```json
{
  "subject": "Welcome, \{{{ first_name }}}",
  "body": {
    "type": "plain_text",
    "plain_text": { "text": "Thanks for joining Acme." }
  }
}
```

### Raw HTML email

```json
{
  "subject": "Your receipt #\{{{ order.id }}}",
  "from_name": "Acme Billing",
  "from_address": "billing@acme.com",
  "reply_to": "support@acme.com",
  "body": {
    "type": "raw",
    "preheader": "Order summary attached",
    "raw": {
      "html": "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"></head><body><h1>Thanks for your order</h1><p>Total: \{{ order.total_formatted }}</p></body></html>",
      "text": "Thanks for your order. Total: \{{{ order.total_formatted }}}"
    }
  }
}
```

The HTML is a full document so the `preheader` has a `<body>` tag to attach to. `from_address` / `reply_to` take effect only with your own email vendor (see the verified behaviour list below).

## Designing a responsive, cross-client email (`raw` body)

When you author a `raw` HTML email as JSON you get **no visual preview** — the HTML has to be responsive and render across clients *by construction*. A good-looking render in one previewer does **not** mean it works in Gmail, Outlook, or on a phone. Follow the rules below and start from the skeleton.

### Rules that decide whether it works

- **Single column, fluid width.** Put `style="width:100%; max-width:600px"` on the content table. **Never use a fixed `width:600px` (or `width="600"` on the table) as the only width** — it overflows on phones (a ~600px email on a ~375px screen) and forces the client to zoom out, shrinking the text. `max-width` lets it shrink to the screen.
- **Tables for layout, inline CSS only.** Gmail strips `<style>` blocks and Yahoo rewrites classes, so put every *layout* style inline. The fluid-hybrid pattern needs no `@media` query; the one exception is the optional dark-mode media query in `<head>` (see Dark mode).
- **Web-safe fonts only.** Arial, Helvetica, Georgia, Tahoma, Verdana, Times New Roman. Custom and Google fonts fall back to system defaults everywhere except Apple Mail.
- **Readable type.** Body 14–16px (never below 14), headings 22–28px, `line-height` ~1.5, dark text on a light background.
- **One bulletproof CTA.** Build the button as a table cell with `bgcolor` plus a padded `display:inline-block` anchor (see the skeleton). Keep the tap target ≥ 44px tall, use one primary CTA, and label it with an action verb and outcome ("Open workspace", "View receipt").
- **Images.** Always set `alt` text (clients block images by default) and `style="display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:0;"` so they scale down on mobile. Reference a **public https URL** — never a `data:` URI (Gmail and Outlook block them) and never a local path. Keep the whole email under ~100KB; Gmail clips above ~102KB.
- **Full document + preheader.** Always emit `<html><head></head><body>…</body></html>` so `preheader` and `email_markup` have tags to attach to. Make the hidden preheader `<div>` the first element inside `<body>`.
- **Footer + unsubscribe.** Small muted text with a working unsubscribe link: `\{{{$hosted_preference_url}}}` (CAN-SPAM / GDPR).

### Outlook on Windows (the client that breaks layouts)

Outlook for Windows renders with Word's engine: it **ignores `max-width` and `border-radius`**, and `<div>` layouts are unreliable. To keep Outlook at a fixed 600px while every other client stays fluid, wrap the content table in an **MSO "ghost table"** — a `<table>` inside `<!--[if mso]>` conditional comments that only Outlook sees:

```html
<!--[if mso]><table role="presentation" width="600" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0"><tr><td><![endif]-->
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:100%; max-width:600px;">
  … your content …
</table>
<!--[if mso]></td></tr></table><![endif]-->
```

Outlook reads the fixed-600 ghost table and ignores the fluid `max-width` one; Apple Mail, Gmail, Yahoo, and phones ignore the MSO comments and use the fluid table. Rounded corners still render square in Outlook — that is acceptable graceful degradation, so never rely on `border-radius` for meaning.

### Complete responsive skeleton (copy it, then fill the content cells)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge">
  <title>Your workspace is ready</title>
</head>
<body style="margin:0; padding:0; background-color:#f2f4f7;">
  <div style="display:none; max-height:0; overflow:hidden; mso-hide:all;">\{{workspace_name}} is ready — invite your team.</div>
  <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="background-color:#f2f4f7;">
    <tr>
      <td align="center" style="padding:24px 12px;">
        <!--[if mso]><table role="presentation" width="600" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0"><tr><td><![endif]-->
        <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; background-color:#ffffff; border-radius:12px; font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;">
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 32px 8px;">
              <img src="\{{$tenant.logo}}" alt="\{{default $tenant.name "Acme"}}" height="26" style="height:26px; display:block; border:0;">
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:24px 32px 8px;">
              <h1 style="margin:0; font-size:24px; line-height:32px; color:#101828; font-weight:bold;">Your workspace is ready, \{{first_name}}</h1>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:8px 32px 24px;">
              <p style="margin:0; font-size:16px; line-height:26px; color:#475467;"><strong>\{{workspace_name}}</strong> is set up. Invite your team and send your first notification.</p>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:0 32px 32px;">
              <table role="presentation" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0">
                <tr>
                  <td align="center" bgcolor="\{{default $tenant.primary_color "#4F46E5"}}" style="border-radius:8px;">
                    <a href="\{{{action_url}}}" style="display:inline-block; padding:14px 28px; font-size:16px; line-height:20px; font-weight:bold; color:#ffffff; text-decoration:none; border-radius:8px;">Open \{{workspace_name}}</a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:0 32px 32px; border-top:1px solid #eaecf0;">
              <p style="margin:16px 0 0; font-size:12px; line-height:18px; color:#98a2b3;">\{{default $tenant.name "Acme"}} · \{{default $tenant.properties.address "123 Market St"}}<br>
              You received this because you created a workspace. <a href="\{{{$hosted_preference_url}}}" style="color:#98a2b3; text-decoration:underline;">Unsubscribe or manage preferences</a>.</p>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <!--[if mso]></td></tr></table><![endif]-->
      </td>
    </tr>
  </table>
</body>
</html>
```

Use `\{{ }}` (double braces) for values that go into HTML — it HTML-escapes and keeps injected markup safe — and `\{{{ }}}` (triple) only for URLs (`action_url`, `$hosted_preference_url`) and the subject, preheader, and plain-text part, which are not HTML. See [Handlebars Helpers](handlebars-helpers.md).

### Checklist before you save

- [ ] Content table is `width:100%; max-width:600px` (not a fixed 600px)
- [ ] An MSO ghost table wraps it (Outlook stays fixed-600)
- [ ] All layout CSS is inline (the only `<style>` allowed is the optional dark-mode media query)
- [ ] Web-safe fonts; body ≥ 14px, headings 22–28px, line-height ~1.5
- [ ] One bulletproof CTA, ≥ 44px tall, action-verb label
- [ ] Every image has `alt` plus `max-width:100%; height:auto`, served from a public https URL
- [ ] Full `<html><head></head><body>` document with a hidden preheader
- [ ] Footer has a working `\{{{$hosted_preference_url}}}` unsubscribe link

## Structure and deliverability

A responsive layout is not enough — these decide whether the email reads well and reaches the inbox.

- **Inverted pyramid.** One idea per email: branded header → one clear headline → 2–3 sentences of context → a single primary CTA → footer. Don't bury the CTA or stack competing asks.
- **Set the preheader.** It is the inbox preview line after the subject — 40–140 characters that *extend* the subject, not repeat it. Left blank, clients pull the first body text (often a link or "view in browser"). It needs a `<body>` tag to attach to (see [Body](#body)).
- **Keep the plain-text part useful.** HTML and plain text are both sent. Leave `text` empty to auto-generate it from the HTML, or write your own for OTP, receipt, and outreach emails, where plain text is what many recipients read. A real multipart email also scores better with spam filters.
- **Mind the image-to-text ratio.** Aim for ≤ ~40% images and ≥ 60% live text. Image-only emails land in spam and show nothing when images are blocked — prefer HTML/CSS for headers and always set `alt` text.
- **Unsubscribe and sender.** Every email needs a **visible** unsubscribe link (Gmail and Yahoo require easy opt-out) — put `\{{{$hosted_preference_url}}}`, the per-recipient signed link to SuprSend's hosted preference page, in the footer, not hidden. Use `\{{$embedded_preference_url}}` only when you host your own preference page. Keep a recognisable `from_name`. (Sending domain, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC are vendor setup, not the template.)

## From email to notification: common recipes

A template is only the content. These patterns pair the email with the SuprSend flow that sends it — read the `suprsend-workflow-schema` skill for the workflow JSON.

- **Transactional** (receipt, password reset, invite): one email, sent at once. Author the variant, then start it with `workflows_trigger`, or with `events_track` when an event drives the workflow (a single `send_email` node). Keep promotional content minimal (CAN-SPAM).
- **Welcome / onboarding series:** several emails over days. One workflow with `send_email` nodes separated by `delay` nodes (Day 0 welcome → Day 2 tips → Day 5 nudge), each pointing at its own template. `workflows_save_draft` → `workflows_publish`.
- **Promotional / announcement to many users:** one email to a whole audience. Put the recipients in a list (`lists_upsert`, static or dynamic by query), then send with `broadcasts_trigger`. A dynamic list targets a segment without managing membership by hand.
- **Product update / digest:** batch many items into one email. Use a `digest` node in the workflow, and in the HTML iterate the batched items with a Handlebars `\{{#each}}` loop (Merge-Tag rows are the Design-Editor equivalent).

The design rules above still apply to every recipe. For a notification that spans channels, add a variant per channel (inbox, push, SMS, Slack) to the same template so the message stays consistent everywhere.

## Accessibility and contrast

Readable for everyone, and intact when images are blocked — this also helps deliverability.

- **Live text, not images, for words.** Keep the headline, body, and CTA as real HTML text. Outlook (and often Gmail) block images by default; if an image held your headline or CTA, the email then says nothing. Use images for photos and decoration only.
- **Contrast.** Meet WCAG AA — **4.5:1** for body text, and **3:1** for large text (≥ 24px) and the CTA label on its button. Dark text on light reads best; avoid grey-on-grey.
- **Real headings.** Use `<h1>`–`<h6>` for structure, not a bold `<p>`; screen readers navigate by headings.
- **Layout tables are decoration.** Put `role="presentation"` on every layout `<table>` so screen readers don't read rows and cells as data (the skeleton already does).
- **Language and alt text.** Set `lang` on `<html>`. Give informative images a descriptive `alt`, and decorative images `alt=""` so they are skipped.
- **Never rely on colour alone.** A coloured status word also needs text or an icon.

## Dark mode

Dark mode is a default for a large share of readers, and clients differ — some keep your colours, some partially invert, some fully invert. Design so the email survives all three.

- **Build a solid light-mode email first, then layer dark styles on top.**
- **Declare support and add a dark override** in `<head>` (honoured by Apple Mail and a few others; most clients auto-adjust instead). This `<style>` block is the one allowed exception to inline-only CSS — keep all *layout* CSS inline, and let Gmail strip this block and fall back gracefully:

```html
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light dark">
<style>
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .email-bg   { background:#121212 !important; }
    .email-card { background:#1e1e1e !important; }
    .email-text { color:#e6e6e6 !important; }
  }
</style>
```

Add the matching classes (`email-bg`, `email-card`, `email-text`) to the elements in the skeleton.

- **Recolour every coloured text element, not just the card.** The dark media query only recolours elements that carry a class it targets. An inline `color:` on a `<strong>`, a wordmark, a price, or a table cell with no class keeps its light-mode colour — so dark text on the now-dark card turns invisible. Give every coloured text element a dark-mode class (or a colour that works on both backgrounds).
- **Avoid pure `#000000` and `#FFFFFF`** — use a soft dark (~`#121212`) and an off-white so auto-inversion does not look harsh.
- **Logos:** use a transparent PNG; if the logo is dark, add light padding or a subtle stroke so it stays visible on a dark background (a pure-black logo disappears).
- **Test both modes** in Apple Mail and the Gmail app before publishing.

## Buttons and images

- **CTA copy.** Label the button with an action and its outcome — "Open workspace", "View your invoice" — never "Click here". One primary CTA per email (repeat it at the bottom only for a long email); keep it 200–300px wide and ≥ 44px tall.
- **Host images on SuprSend.** Upload with `assets_upload` and use the returned URL. Never use a `data:` URI (Gmail and Outlook block them) or a local path. Tenant branding (`\{{$tenant.logo}}`) is already hosted.
- **Image specs.** Full-width ≤ 600px (a header is 600×200–300); supply a **2× retina** source (1200px for a 600px slot); set `style="display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:0;"` so it scales on mobile; keep the whole email under ~100KB (Gmail clips ~102KB). Prefer HTML/CSS over images for simple headers.

## Verified behaviour (overrides the docs below)

- **Plain-text part:** if `text` is empty, it's generated from the rendered HTML **at send time** (not at commit).
- **`from_address` and `reply_to` are ignored** when the email is sent through SuprSend's built-in email vendor; they apply only with your own email vendor.
- **`extra_to`, `cc`, `bcc`, `reply_to`** are comma-separated address strings. If any one address in a list is malformed after rendering, that **entire list is silently dropped** at send time.
- **`preheader` / `email_markup`** need a `<body>` tag / a `</head>` tag in the HTML respectively, and are ignored for `plain_text` bodies (see [Body](#body)).
- **Updates merge with stored content:** omitted top-level keys keep their stored values, and `body` is merged key by key — `body.raw`, `body.designer` and `body.plain_text` are each merged field by field. Switching `body.type` keeps the other types' stored content. To clear a field, send it explicitly as `""`.

